--TEST--
`Core\Bytes::pack` reports which argument each mismatch is about, and every code accepts exactly one kind of argument — spec § 7, `rule:types/bytes` and `rule:types/conversion`, `rule:types/grammar`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Spec § 7's `pack(string $format, mixed ...$values): bytes`. The value list is
// `mixed`, which is `rule:types/grammar`'s one unchecked position, so nothing the
// checker knows lines the format up against the arguments — the member does it
// at the call, and `rule:types/conversion`'s checked-never-repaired rule is why every
// mismatch is a throw rather than a NUL, a truncation or a wrap.
//
// `tests/conformance/core/bytes-pack-writes-explicit-width-fields.nvst` pins
// what a well-formed call writes and reports its refusals as "threw". This case
// pins the six refusals themselves, by the message each names, because a member
// that threw the *wrong* one still reads as correct there.
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/bytes.rs` owns each site.

final class Pack {
    // The message a refused call reports, or "" when the call is an answer.
    // Each `catch` binds its own name: a binding belongs to the function, so
    // two clauses in one class body cannot share one.
    public static function none(string $format): string {
        try {
            Core\Bytes::pack($format);
            return "";
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            return $e->message;
        }
    }

    public static function one(string $format, mixed $a): string {
        try {
            Core\Bytes::pack($format, $a);
            return "";
        } catch (Throwable $b) {
            return $b->message;
        }
    }

    public static function two(string $format, mixed $a, mixed $c): string {
        try {
            Core\Bytes::pack($format, $a, $c);
            return "";
        } catch (Throwable $d) {
            return $d->message;
        }
    }

    public static function accepts(string $format, mixed $a): string {
        if (Pack::one($format, $a) == "") {
            return "y";
        }
        return "n";
    }

    // Which of the seven argument kinds the code writes a field from, in the
    // order `string`, `bytes`, `int`, `uint`, `float`, `bool`, `null`.
    public static function mask(string $code): string {
        return Pack::accepts($code, "A")
            . Pack::accepts($code, "A" as bytes)
            . Pack::accepts($code, 65)
            . Pack::accepts($code, 65 as uint)
            . Pack::accepts($code, 65.0)
            . Pack::accepts($code, true)
            . Pack::accepts($code, null);
    }
}

// A field the call wrote no argument for names the code that came up short and
// both counts, so a format built at run time says where it outran the call
// rather than only that it did. The repeater and the buffer codes reach the
// same site.
echo Pack::one("NN", 1), "\n";
echo Pack::one("N2", 1), "\n";
echo Pack::one("a4a4", "Hi"), "\n";

// The other side of that bound is its own refusal, not a silent drop: a format
// that writes fewer fields than the call passed reports both counts too.
echo Pack::two("N", 1, 2), "\n";
echo "[", Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Bytes::pack("NN", 1, 2)), "]\n";

// `*` is the one repeater that can meet neither bound — it takes exactly the
// arguments left, down to none at all — so a `*` format never reaches either
// counting refusal.
echo "[", Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Bytes::pack("N*", 1, 2)), "]\n";
echo "[", Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Bytes::pack("N*")), "]\n";

// `x` is the exception in the other direction: it consumes no argument, so its
// repeater is a NUL count and `*` has nothing to count.
echo "[", Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Bytes::pack("x")), "]\n";
echo "[", Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Bytes::pack("x2")), "]\n";
echo Pack::none("x*"), "\n";

// A field given the wrong kind of argument names the kind of field it writes
// and the runtime tag the program actually passed — the tag `mixed` carries,
// not one the checker placed.
echo Pack::one("a4", 65), "\n";
echo Pack::one("Z", 65.0), "\n";
echo Pack::one("N", "x"), "\n";
echo Pack::one("C", 65.0), "\n";
echo Pack::one("G", 1), "\n";
echo Pack::one("e", "1.0"), "\n";

// The claim those six rows are samples of, counted over the whole roster: each
// code accepts exactly one class of argument and refuses the other five kinds,
// so the three classes partition the codes. A code that grew a conversion of
// its own — an integer field taking a numeral string, a float field taking an
// `int` — still looks right on its own line and fails here. `x` is the code
// that accepts nothing, since it reads no argument at all.
array<string> $codes = ["a", "A", "Z", "C", "n", "N", "v", "V", "J", "P", "G", "g", "E", "e", "x"];
int $buffer = 0;
int $integer = 0;
int $fractional = 0;
int $refuses = 0;
foreach ($codes as string $code) {
    string $m = Pack::mask($code);
    if ($m == "yynnnnn") {
        $buffer = $buffer + 1;
    }
    if ($m == "nnyynnn") {
        $integer = $integer + 1;
    }
    if ($m == "nnnnynn") {
        $fractional = $fractional + 1;
    }
    if ($m == "nnnnnnn") {
        $refuses = $refuses + 1;
    }
}
echo $buffer, " ", $integer, " ", $fractional, " ", $refuses, " of ", Core\Arr::count($codes), "\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Bytes::pack: `N` wants argument 2, and the call wrote 1
Core\Bytes::pack: `N` wants argument 2, and the call wrote 1
Core\Bytes::pack: `a` wants argument 2, and the call wrote 1
Core\Bytes::pack: the format writes 1 of the 2 arguments the call wrote
[0000000100000002]
[0000000100000002]
[]
[00]
[0000]
Core\Bytes::pack: `x*` has nothing to repeat — `x` consumes no argument, so write the number of NUL bytes
Core\Bytes::pack: `a` writes a buffer field, and the argument is a Int
Core\Bytes::pack: `Z` writes a buffer field, and the argument is a Float
Core\Bytes::pack: `N` writes an integer field, and the argument is a Str
Core\Bytes::pack: `C` writes an integer field, and the argument is a Float
Core\Bytes::pack: `G` writes a float field, and the argument is a Int
Core\Bytes::pack: `e` writes a float field, and the argument is a Str
3 7 4 1 of 15
